cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  Please cite this version of the software when using its integral or ring
  extension or Lean proof. Cite the original OpenAI construction and the
  earlier all-fields extension separately. Creator credit records human
  direction; the new proof development and manuscript are AI-generated.
type: software
title: 'The Matrix Multiplication Bound ω ≤ 9/4 over Associative Rings'
version: 0.1.0
authors:
  - family-names: Navot
    given-names: Sela
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0001-8002-5835'
repository-code: 'https://github.com/selanavot/omega_le_nine_quarters_all_rings'
url: 'https://github.com/selanavot/omega_le_nine_quarters_all_rings'
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  A Lean formalization establishing that the matrix multiplication exponent
  is at most 9/4 over every associative unital ring, including noncommutative
  rings. The stronger intermediate theorem bounds the exponent of exact
  integer coefficient tensor rank by 9/4. For every positive epsilon, the
  development proves correct arithmetic programs using at most
  C n^(9/4+epsilon) operations. An explicit zero-cost program handles the
  trivial ring, whose real-valued exponent is zero by the unchanged infimum
  convention. OpenAI supplied the numerical bound, main proof architecture
  and arithmetic-program specification. This project builds on the earlier
  all-fields extension. The new proof development and accompanying manuscript
  are AI-generated with Codex under Sela Navot's human direction.
  Compilation, axiom inspection, fresh-kernel replay
  of the six-claim dependency closure, and frozen-specification Comparator
  with all six bundled checkers passed locally. No human peer review,
  improved numerical exponent, exact O(n^(9/4)) endpoint, efficient uniform
  circuit generator or bit-complexity bound is claimed. Five vendored
  fixed-point proof modules retain their separate MIT license.
keywords:
  - matrix multiplication
  - arithmetic complexity
  - associative rings
  - integer tensor rank
  - Lean
  - formal verification
